<SubSection title="Thermodynamic Laws & Conservation Principles" id="Thermodynamic Laws & Conservation Principles">

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
    <CalloutCard title="Remember!">
            <p>
                Temperature and heat are related but not the same thing. 
Temperature is a property of an object or system that describes the average kinetic energy of the particles within it. 
Heat refers specifically to the transferring of thermal energy across a system boundary due to a temperature difference. 
            </p>
    </CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>

<h4 style="text-align: center;">
  <strong>Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics: Thermal Equillibrium</strong>
</h4>

<p> The zeroth law of thermodynamics establishes the concept of thermal equilibrium and provides us a foundation for defining and measuring temperature. 
Specifically, it states that if two systems are in thermal equilibrium with a third system, then they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. 
</p>

<p> Although this law may seem simple in wording, it plays a crucial role in understanding heat transfer. 
It identifies that temperature is a shared property of systems in equilibrium and explains why temperature can be compared consistently between different objects.
</p>

<p>Example: The law also provides the physical justification for the use of thermometers. 
A thermometer acts as a third system: when it is placed in contact with an object and allowed to reach thermal equilibrium, the thermometer and the object share the same temperature. 
</p>

<p> When two objects are in thermal equilibrium, there is no net heat transfer between them. 
If a temperature difference exists, then heat will flow until thermal equilibrium is reached. 
Therefore, the zeroth law essentially defines the condition under which we expect heat transfer to stop as thermal equillibrium.
</p>

<CalloutCard title="Did you know?">
        <u class="m-0">Callout Card</u>
<p>While the Zeroth law of thermodynamics was established in the 1930's after the First and Second laws, it was named the Zeroth law
to recognize its foundational nature in defining both temperature and thermal equillibrium.</p>
    </CalloutCard>

<h4 style="text-align: center;">
  <strong>First Law of Thermodynamics: Conservation of Energy</strong>
</h4>

<p> The first law of thermodynamics expresses the principle of energy conservation. 
It says that energy cannot be created or destroyed and can only be transferred or converted between forms. 
This law is incredibly important in understanding all situations of heat transfer. 
</p>

<p> In thermal systems, the first law explains why temperature change occurs. 
A system’s temperature can increase only if thermal energy enters the system or is generated internally, 
and it can only decrease if thermal energy leaves the system. 
Temperature changes as a consequence of energy transfer or conversion and it won’t change spontaneously. 
</p>

<p>
When we apply the first law to heat transfer problems, we usually express it as an energy balance, 
which balances energy entering the system, energy leaving the system, internal heat heat generation, and energy stored within the system.
Representing the first law of thermodynamics as an energy balance equation will be an important step for deriving the governing
heat equation.
</p>

<h4 style="text-align: center;">
  <strong>Second Law of Thermodynamics: Directionality of Heat Transfer</strong>
</h4>

<p> The second law of thermodynamics is important in understanding the directionality of heat transfer. 
It states that in an isolated system, the total energy of the system can only increase or remain constant over time. 
</p>

<p>  In heat transfer, this explains why heat transfer only spontaneously occurs from regions of higher temperature to regions of lower temperature. 
This type of transfer increases the total entropy of the system in alignment with the second law.
</p>

</SubSection>




<SubSection title="Conduction" id="Conduction">
<p> Conductive heat transfer is the transport of thermal energy through a medium due to temperature gradients within the material. Energy is transferred down the temperature gradient. The method of heat transfer is called diffusion. Diffusion is the net transfer of energy due to random molecular motion. 
</p>
<p> Fourier’s equation is used to describe conductive heat transfer. You can calculate heat flow and heat flux using Fourier's equation. 
</p>
<p> Heat Flow: </p>
<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{q_x}{A} = -k \\frac{dT}{dx}" title="" />
<p> Heat Flux: </p>
<DisplayEquation equation="q^{''}_x = -k \\frac{dT}{dx}" title="" />
<p> Lets go over the variables present in these equations: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item><InlineEquation equation="q_x (W)" /> = rate of heat flow in the x direction </Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="A (m^2)" /> = area perpendicular to the direction of heat flow</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="q^{''}_x (\\frac{W}{m^2})" /> = rate of heat flow per unit area</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T (C or K)" /> = temperature of the medium at x</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="k (\\frac{W}{mC} or \\frac{W}{mK})" /> = thermal conductivity of the medium</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Steady State" id="Steady State">
<p> In steady state heat conduction, temperature does not change with time. However, temperature can change with positon and this is what drives the transfer of heat. There are three main examples of steady state heat conduction: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item>In a slab</Item>
<Item>In a cylinder</Item>
<Item>With heat generation (Q)</Item>

</Itemize>

</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Unsteady State" id="Unsteady State">
<p> In unsteady state heat conduction, temperature changes over time. There are three main examples of unsteady state heat conduction: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item>With no spatial variation</Item>
<Item>With semi-infinite geometry</Item>
<Item>With finite geometry</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSection title="Convection" id="Convection">
<p> Convective heat transfer is the transport of thermal energy due to bulk motion of a fluid over a surface. </p>
<p> The equation that describes convection is </p>
<DisplayEquation equation="q_{1-2} = hA(T_1 - T_2)" title="" />
<p> Lets go over the variables present in this equation: </p>
<Itemize>

<Item><InlineEquation equation="q_{1-2} (W)" /> = rate of heat flow from 1 to 2 </Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="A (m^2)" /> = area normal to the direction of heat flow</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_1 - T_2 (C)" /> = temperature difference between the surface and fluid</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="h (\\frac{W}{m^2C})" /> = convective heat transfer coefficient (this coefficient always includes the effect of conduction in the fluid)</Item>

</Itemize>
</SubSection>

